---
import Footer from "../../components/Footer.astro";
import Header from "../../components/Header.astro";
import { fetchGithubStars, formatStarCount } from "../../github";
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";

const stars = await fetchGithubStars();
const starsLabel = stars === null ? "Star" : formatStarCount(stars);
---

<BaseLayout
  title="CRM integrations · Rakazo"
  description="Connect websites, newsletter forms, automation platforms, and AI clients to the Manor CRM with REST, webhooks, OpenAPI, and MCP."
>
  <div class="container">
    <Header starsLabel={starsLabel} />
    <main id="main" class="integration-docs">
      <aside class="integration-docs__nav" aria-label="On this page">
        <span>On this page</span>
        <a href="#choose">Choose a surface</a>
        <a href="#credentials">Credentials</a>
        <a href="#upsert">Newsletter signup</a>
        <a href="#pull">Pull changes</a>
        <a href="#webhooks">Webhooks</a>
        <a href="#mcp">MCP</a>
        <a href="#openapi">OpenAPI</a>
      </aside>

      <article class="integration-docs__copy">
        <p class="integration-docs__eyebrow">Developer documentation</p>
        <h1>Connect anything to the CRM</h1>
        <p class="integration-docs__lede">
          Websites, newsletter forms, automation platforms, and AI clients can all work with the
          same workspace-scoped CRM used by Manor on web, desktop, and mobile.
        </p>

        <section id="choose">
          <h2>Choose the right surface</h2>
          <div class="integration-docs__surface-grid">
            <div><strong>REST</strong><span>Deterministic reads and writes between systems.</span></div>
            <div><strong>Webhooks</strong><span>Push CRM changes to your application.</span></div>
            <div><strong>MCP</strong><span>Give CRM tools to an AI client.</span></div>
            <div><strong>OpenAPI</strong><span>Import the contract into automation tools.</span></div>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section id="credentials">
          <h2>Create a machine credential</h2>
          <p>
            In Manor on web or desktop, open <strong>Integrations → CRM API</strong>. Name the
            credential, select the smallest scopes it needs, and choose <strong>Create token</strong>.
            Copy the returned <code>manor_…</code> token immediately. Manor stores only its hash.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><code>crm:read</code> lists and searches CRM records.</li>
            <li><code>crm:write</code> creates and updates CRM records.</li>
            <li><code>webhooks:manage</code> manages webhook endpoints through the API.</li>
          </ul>
          <div class="integration-docs__callout">
            Keep tokens on a trusted backend or in an automation platform's encrypted secret store.
            Never embed one in browser JavaScript, a mobile app, or a public repository.
          </div>
        </section>

        <section id="upsert">
          <h2>Send a newsletter signup</h2>
          <p>
            The upsert endpoint links the source system's stable subscriber ID to a Manor contact.
            If that identity is new, Manor can match an existing contact by email before creating one.
          </p>
          <pre><code>{`curl https://your-manor.example/v1/crm/contacts/upsert \\
  --request POST \\
  --header "Authorization: Bearer $MANOR_TOKEN" \\
  --header "Content-Type: application/json" \\
  --header "Idempotency-Key: newsletter-signup-1042" \\
  --data '{
    "source": "website-newsletter",
    "external_id": "subscriber-1042",
    "first_name": "Ada",
    "last_name": "Lovelace",
    "email": "ada@example.com",
    "tags": ["newsletter", "website"]
  }'`}</code></pre>
          <p>
            Repeating the same request is safe. External identities resolve to the canonical contact,
            while <code>Idempotency-Key</code> replays the completed response for 24 hours. Reusing a
            key with a different body returns <code>409</code>.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section id="pull">
          <h2>Pull contact changes</h2>
          <pre><code>{`GET /v1/crm/contacts?updated_after=2026-08-27T12:00:00Z&limit=100
Authorization: Bearer $MANOR_TOKEN`}</code></pre>
          <p>
            Follow <code>next_cursor</code> until it is null. The cursor includes the update timestamp
            and record ID, so records sharing the same timestamp are not skipped.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section id="webhooks">
          <h2>Receive webhook events</h2>
          <p>
            Add an HTTPS endpoint under <strong>Integrations → CRM API</strong>. Copy its one-time
            signing secret. Manor currently exposes <code>contact.created</code> and
            <code>contact.updated</code> in the panel and retries failed deliveries with exponential
            backoff up to eight attempts.
          </p>
          <pre><code>{`const expected = createHmac("sha256", signingSecret)
  .update(timestamp + "." + rawBody)
  .digest("hex");

timingSafeEqual(
  Buffer.from(signature.replace("v1=", ""), "hex"),
  Buffer.from(expected, "hex"),
);`}</code></pre>
          <p>
            Verify <code>X-Manor-Signature</code> against the raw request body before parsing it, and
            reject stale <code>X-Manor-Timestamp</code> values to prevent replay.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section id="mcp">
          <h2>Connect an MCP client</h2>
          <p>
            Point a Streamable HTTP client at <code>/mcp/crm</code> and use the machine token as a
            bearer credential. A read-only token only receives read tools.
          </p>
          <pre><code>{`{
  "mcpServers": {
    "manor-crm": {
      "url": "https://your-manor.example/mcp/crm",
      "headers": {
        "Authorization": "Bearer manor_replace_me"
      }
    }
  }
}`}</code></pre>
          <p>
            Use <code>crm_sync_contact</code> for source-aware synchronization. The remaining
            <code>crm_*</code> tools cover contact search, deals, pipelines, and stage movement.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section id="openapi">
          <h2>Import the OpenAPI contract</h2>
          <p>
            Every deployment publishes its machine-readable contract at
            <code>/v1/openapi.json</code>. Import it when a platform supports OpenAPI but not MCP.
          </p>
        </section>
      </article>
    </main>
    <Footer />
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
